Dials NEWS                                    -*- outline -*-

* Noteworthy changes in release ?.? (????-??-??) [?]

** New features

  A full-screen interface with a menu bar (F9), a function-key bar,
  pages to switch between with 1-9, Alt-1..9 and Ctrl-PgUp/PgDn, and
  four color themes: classic, blue, dark and mono.

  The Overview page shows the host and system, uptime, load, and the
  use of the processors, memory and swap with their recent history.

  The CPU page shows how the processors' time is used, the use and
  frequency of each processor, the temperature, load, pressure and the
  rates of context switches, interrupts and new processes.

  The Memory page divides memory between programs, the kernel, buffers
  and cache, and shows swap, zram, paging rates, memory pressure and
  the processes with the most memory.

  The Disks page shows the throughput, busy time, latency and
  temperature of each disk, and the space on each local file system.

  The Network page shows each interface's state, speed, traffic,
  addresses and history, and counts connections by state.

  The Processes page lists the processes as a list or a tree, sorted by
  processor use, memory, PID, user, name or time, and filtered by
  name.  F7 changes a process's nice value and F8 sends it a signal.

  The Sensors page lists every hardware monitoring sensor with its
  lowest and highest readings and its limit, and graphs one of them.

  The Power page shows batteries, power supplies, processor power from
  RAPL and power readings from hardware monitoring.

  Two pages can be shown side by side: 's' shows one panel or two, Tab
  moves between them and Ctrl-U swaps them.

  Options > Save setup keeps the interval, theme, panels and process
  sorting in dials/dials.conf under $XDG_CONFIG_HOME or ~/.config.

  'l' or --log=FILE logs each sample to a CSV file, or to JSON lines
  for files ending in .json or .jsonl.

  The GPU page lists NVIDIA, AMD and Intel graphics processors with
  their temperature, utilization, video memory, power, clocks and fan
  speed, graphs of their history, and the processes using them.  NVIDIA
  GPUs are read through the NVIDIA driver's NVML library, loaded at run
  time, so Dials builds and runs on systems without it; AMD and Intel
  GPUs are read from the kernel's drivers.

  The --demo option shows simulated data, and --once prints a
  plain-text report instead of starting the full-screen interface.
  The --sysroot option reads a saved copy of /proc and /sys, for
  testing.

